content:
An email popped up in my inbox today: &ldquo;You can just build things, enjoy 6 months on us.&rdquo;
Sender: the Codex team at OpenAI. The first line, in big letters: &ldquo;You&rsquo;re in.&rdquo; Then: &ldquo;Thanks for maintaining open source projects.&rdquo;
I stared at that line three times and couldn&rsquo;t help laughing out loud.
Free ride: secured A while back I filled out their Codex for Open Source application on a whim. Didn&rsquo;t expect much — I just listed the PRs I&rsquo;d submitted to context-mode, plus my own byf and dev-skills.
Quick math: ChatGPT Pro is $200 a month, so half a year is $1,200. Honestly, saving that money feels great, but what made me happiest was actually this line from the email: &ldquo;reviewing changes, maintaining releases, keeping quality high.&rdquo;
The most annoying part of open source was never the coding — it&rsquo;s reading a stranger&rsquo;s badly formatted PR in the middle of the night, debugging issues you can&rsquo;t reproduce, and re-testing piles of fiddly edge cases before every release. None of that turns into a slick demo video, and none of it is worth flexing on Twitter. The best treatment you normally get is an emoji reaction on an issue, or a flat &ldquo;thx.&rdquo;
And today, someone said &ldquo;thank you for maintaining open source&rdquo; formally, in writing, and threw in six months of Pro on top.
It&rsquo;s a noreply address, so I can&rsquo;t write back — but the mood is genuinely great. Codex gets to run hard for the next six months. Now, back to fixing issues.
